:"triaged-evidence-bump","color":"ededed","default":false,"description":"Issue-sweep disposition: evidence bump"}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nicsuzor/academicOps/milestones/1","html_url":"https://github.com/nicsuzor/academicOps/milestone/1","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nicsuzor/academicOps/milestones/1/labels","id":16034952,"node_id":"MI_kwDORj7DXM4A9KyI","number":1,"title":"v0.4 release","description":"","creator":{"login":"nicsuzor","id":4496978,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ0OTY5Nzg=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4496978?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor","html_url":"https://github.com/nicsuzor","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nicsuzor/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"open_issues":15,"closed_issues":6,"state":"open","created_at":"2026-05-22T00:44:32Z","updated_at":"2026-05-24T04:56:24Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":null},"comments":43,"created_at":"2026-05-17T07:33:23Z","updated_at":"2026-07-07T05:42:45Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Cluster-level forensic — six same-family corrections in one day\n\n**A17 recusal: forensic only. Names the structural shape; does not propose a fix.**\n\n### The cluster\n\nSix corrections, one coordinator (junior in cowork), one day (2026-05-15 → 16):\n\n1. **#1090** — verdict rubber-stamping. Coordinator passed a delegated-subagent verdict through to the user without forming its own position.\n2. **#1115** — option-menu abdication. Coordinator surfaced a 3-option menu to the user on a framework-meta question that authority-delegation docs would have resolved.\n3. **#1114** — doc-incoherence on polecat capabilities. Coordinator mechanically read two surfaces (WORKERS.md and AGENT-TOOLS.md) without reconciling them or noting the contradiction.\n4. **#1115 volume-bump** — worker-class substitution as hack. When dispatch surfaced an underlying capability gap, coordinator reached for \"substitute a different worker\" instead of naming the bug.\n5. **PKB-search-vs-grep** (this session) — defaulted to `grep`/filesystem mechanics for discovery when `search` / `task_search` / `pkb_context` were the right primitives.\n6. **Treemap QA criteria** (this session) — produced a 3-layer rubric with 8 binary checks and a 25-point ≥20 threshold when the user had explicitly and repeatedly asked for **qualitative narrative from the user perspective**. The epic body said marsha must \"assess it all from the users perspective, using the dashboard\\s user story and requirements\". The `verify` skill — explicitly labelled \"QA verification, qualitative assessment, criteria design\" — was not consulted. `verify/references/qualitative-assessment.md` contains a complete anti-pattern table whose top row is **\"Pass/Fail tables — Reduces nuance to binary; evaluator stops thinking — Instead: Narrative evaluation on a quality spectrum\"**, and a second row **\"Point scoring — Creates false precision; 73/100 means nothing — Instead: Qualitative judgment with evidence\"**. The coordinator produced exactly the two artifacts the skill names as anti-patterns.\n\n### Load-bearing structural finding\n\n**The coordinator collapses judgment-calls into mechanical operations.**\n\nEvery correction in the cluster is the same shape:\n\n| Coordinator work (judgment) | What the coordinator produced instead (mechanism) |\n|---|---|\n| Form a verdict | Rubber-stamp the subagent's verdict |\n| Resolve a meta-question | Hand the user an option menu |\n| Reconcile two specs | Quote both surfaces side by side |\n| Diagnose a capability gap | Substitute a different worker |\n| Discover relevant prior knowledge | Grep the filesystem |\n| Assess fitness-for-purpose | Build a scorecard |\n\nThe unifying shape: when the coordinator owes the user **a position** (synthesised, narrated, defended), it ships **the shape of a coordinator output** (binary, structured, easily-evaluated) instead. The output looks like coordinator work — it has the right surface features (a verdict slot, a recommendation slot, a criteria slot) — but the actual judgment-formation step is skipped or outsourced to mechanism.\n\nThis is structurally identical to **criterion substitution** as named in marsha.md (\"Main agents unconsciously substitute easier-to-verify criteria\"), but applied to coordinator outputs rather than verification outputs. The coordinator substitutes an **easier-to-produce output shape** for the harder one the user actually asked for.\n\n### Why the framework lets this happen (causal-chain candidates, not fixes)\n\nFour candidate seams where the reflex lives. Each is consistent with the observed failures; the actual fix-direction needs design-layer work outside this forensic.\n\n**(a) verify-skill visibility gap.** `aops-core:verify` exists and contains a complete qualitative-assessment methodology that names the exact anti-pattern the coordinator committed. Junior.md routes \"QA verification\" to \"Agent: marsha / Skill: verify\" — a one-line table entry. The coordinator never invoked it. The skill's rich content (persona immersion, scenario design, narrative-prose output, anti-pattern table) is **not surfaced as a default discipline** at the moment of QA criteria design — it's only discoverable by an agent that already knows it should be doing qualitative assessment and that the verify skill is where the methodology lives. The coordinator did neither.\n\n**(b) marsha-contract shape gap.** marsha.md prescribes verdicts as `PASS / FAIL / REVISE` — explicitly three-state and binary-per-state. The supervisor SKILL's marsha contract reinforces this: \"Marsha returns a one-line verdict\". There is no slot in marsha's contract for qualitative narrative; the contract is shaped to be cheap-to-consume by a supervisor tick. When a coordinator designs criteria *for* marsha, the coordinator naturally produces criteria that fit the verdict shape marsha is contracted to return. The verify skill says \"evaluate fitness-for-purpose in narrative prose\"; marsha's frontmatter says \"Produces PASS/FAIL/REVISE verdicts\". The two surfaces disagree, and the marsha-surface — being terser and closer to the dispatch site — wins.\n\n**(c) rubric-is-cheaper cognitive-load gap.** Rubrics are *cheaper to produce* than narratives. A rubric can be authored by pattern-matching the spec (\"for each requirement, add a checkbox\"). A qualitative narrative requires inhabiting the user, walking scenarios, forming a judgment, and writing prose that defends it. Under any cost pressure (long context, autonomous loop, accumulated session load), the coordinator will reach for the cheaper shape unless something in the framework actively pulls it toward the more expensive one. Nothing currently does.\n\n**(d) meta-gap: the shape is one structural failure, observed six ways.** All six corrections are instances of `judgment → mechanism` substitution. They look like six separate problems (verdict, menu, doc, hack, search, rubric) because they surface at six different work-sites. But the *generative rule* — \"when in doubt, ship the structured artifact, not the judgment\" — is one rule. Filing six separate bugs and patching each work-site one-by-one would treat the symptoms; the structural finding is that **the coordinator role definition does not name \"form and defend a position\" as the load-bearing output**, and so the coordinator optimises toward output-shapes that look correct without requiring that step.\n\n### Why this is epic-level, not another volume-bump on #1115\n\n#1115 names a specific instance: option-menu abdication on a framework-meta question. Its body and remedy-space are scoped to authority-delegation discoverability. Volume-bumping it would file the treemap-QA incident under the wrong shape — the treemap-QA failure is not about authority delegation, it's about the same structural reflex applied to assessment design.\n\nThe cluster has now appeared in six different work-domains: verdict acceptance, meta-question resolution, doc reading, dispatch routing, knowledge discovery, and criteria design. That's breadth sufficient to name the shape as the underlying issue rather than treating each surface separately. Each existing issue (#1090, #1114, #1115) can stay open as a specific-surface bug; this epic-level forensic is the parent that names what they have in common.\n\n### Specific evidence from the latest incident (treemap QA)\n\n- User request, multiple times across the cycle: \"qualitative assessment from the user's perspective\".\n- Epic body for marsha: \"assess it all from the user's perspective, using the dashboard's user story and requirements\".\n- Coordinator produced: 3-layer rubric (Spec adherence / ADHD principles / Graph rubric), 8 binary checklist criteria, 25-point score, ≥20 pass threshold.\n- `verify` skill (`aops-core/skills/verify/SKILL.md`) and its `qualitative-assessment.md` reference: not consulted. The reference contains the anti-pattern table whose first two rows describe exactly what the coordinator produced.\n- User correction: \"we need to adjust your entire approach to QA. I was about to try to apply the criteria you developed, but I can't. they're mechanical, completely geared towards a checkbox and not a qualitative assessment, which is what i specifically asked for.\"\n\n### What this forensic does NOT do\n\nPer A17 recusal: this forensic names the structural shape. It does not propose:\n- where the fix should live (junior.md? verify skill discoverability? marsha contract redesign? a coordinator-side reflex check?)\n- how the framework should pull coordinators toward judgment-shape outputs\n- whether the marsha PASS/FAIL/REVISE contract should change\n- whether `verify` should be auto-invoked on any criteria-design request\n\nThose are design-layer decisions for a separate session.\n\n### Links\n\n- Sibling cluster issues: #1090, #1114, #1115\n- Relevant framework surfaces:\n  - `aops-core/skills/verify/SKILL.md`\n  - `aops-core/skills/verify/references/qualitative-assessment.md`\n  - `aops-core/agents/junior.md` (Routing table; Coordinator mode; Over-deference failure modes)\n  - `aops-core/agents/marsha.md` (PASS/FAIL/REVISE contract; criterion-substitution principle)\n  - `aops-core/skills/supervisor/SKILL.md` (marsha verdict contract; \"one-line verdict\")\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nicsuzor/academicOps/issues/1122/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nicsuzor/academicOps/issues/1122/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1/events","html_url":"https://github.com/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1","id":4825562341,"node_id":"I_kwDOTP9qMs8AAAABH6A85Q","number":1,"title":"Exercise: GitHub Copilot Code Review","user":{"login":"github-actions[bot]","id":41898282,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2026-07-07T05:34:50Z","updated_at":"2026-07-07T05:35:01Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## GitHub Copilot Code Review\n\n<img alt=\"original github octocat\" src=\"https://octodex.github.com/images/original.png\" align=\"left\" height=\"80px\" />\n\n👋 Hey there @Gayeetrin! Welcome to your Skills exercise!\n\nLeverage GitHub Copilot code review to promote code quality and catch issues before they reach production.\n\n---\n\n✨ **This is an interactive, hands-on GitHub Skills exercise!**\n\nAs you complete each step, I’ll leave updates in the comments:\n\n- ✅ Check your work and guide you forward\n- 💡 Share helpful tips and resources\n- 🚀 Celebrate your progress and completion\n\nLet’s get started - good luck and have fun!\n\n<sub>— Mona</sub>\n> <sub> If you encounter any issues along the way please report them [here](https://github.com/skills/copilot-code-review/issues).</sub>","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Gayeetrin/skills-copilot-code-review/issues/1/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249/events","html_url":"https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249","id":4536389319,"node_id":"I_kwDORn_HmM8AAAABDmPOxw","number":249,"title":"Operator Bridge — Active Work Queue / Live Command Log","user":{"login":"antonvdberg-bit","id":264119461,"node_id":"U_kgDOD74kpQ","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/264119461?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit","html_url":"https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/antonvdberg-bit/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":243,"created_at":"2026-05-27T23:13:57Z","updated_at":"2026-07-07T05:23:36Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"<!-- OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1_ISSUE -->\n\nThis issue is the **live coordination surface** for CorpFlow Operator Bridge v1 (Option C — Hybrid).\n\n**Canonical doc on `main`:** [`docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md`](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md)\n\n**Naming (frozen for v1):** [§3 Naming (frozen for v1)](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#3-naming-frozen-for-v1)\n\n### How to use this issue\n\n- **Cursor** posts `Cursor status` comments per [§5.1 Cursor status update](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#51-cursor-status-update).\n- **Anton / ChatGPT-via-Anton** post decisions per [§5.2 Operator / ChatGPT decision](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#52-operator--chatgpt-decision).\n- **Closure records** are mirrored back into `artifacts/chat_history.md` per [§5.3 Closure note](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#53-closure-note-mirrored-to-artifactschathistorymd).\n- **HOLD rules for Cursor:** [§6 When Cursor must HOLD](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#6-when-cursor-must-hold-non-exhaustive).\n- **Operator-only actions:** [§7 When Anton must act](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#7-when-anton-must-act-operator-only).\n- **Guardrails (non-negotiable):** [§9 Guardrails](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/docs/operations/OPERATOR_BRIDGE_V1.md#9-guardrails-non-negotiable).\n\n### Source-of-truth rule\n\n`main` wins over issue comments. If this issue body and the canonical doc disagree, the canonical doc on `main` is authoritative. If the canonical doc moves or its §3 naming changes, an Anton-authored decision comment must update this issue body before Cursor relies on the new naming.\n\n### Hard limits in effect\n\nCursor must not, without explicit per-action authorization:\n\n- merge PRs,\n- touch runtime code, env vars, secrets, DNS, DB, `tenant_id`, analytics, Plausible, Search Console, Telegram behaviour, Vercel config, GitHub settings, or deployment settings,\n- mark work `COMPLETE` unless merged to `main` and verified under [`delivery-reality.mdc`](https://github.com/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/blob/main/.cursor/rules/delivery-reality.mdc),\n- fabricate merge SHAs, deployment IDs, probe results, or production status.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/antonvdberg-bit/corpflow-ai-command-center/issues/249/reactions","to