 | - | #15  | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/cymem/#files) |\n| ✅ | **bitarray** | - | - | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/bitarray/#files) |\n| ✅ | **ml-dtypes** | - | - | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/ml-dtypes/#files) |\n| ✅ | **marisa-trie** | - |  [🔗PR](https://github.com/pytries/marisa-trie/pull/140) | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/marisa-trie/#files) |\n| ✅ | **pycares** | - | [🔗PR](https://github.com/saghul/pycares/pull/233) | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/pycares/#files) |\n| ✅ | **maxminddb** | - | [🔗PR](https://github.com/maxmind/MaxMind-DB-Reader-python/pull/212) | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/maxminddb/#files) |\n| ✅ | **logbook** | - | [🔗PR](https://github.com/getlogbook/logbook/pull/451) | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/Logbook/#files) |\n| ✅ | **wrapt** | - | [🔗PR](https://github.com/GrahamDumpleton/wrapt/pull/290) | - | - | - | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/wrapt/#files) |\n| ✅ | **[frozenlist](https://github.com/aio-libs/frozenlist)** | [🔗Issue](https://github.com/aio-libs/frozenlist/issues/677) | [🔗PR](https://github.com/aio-libs/frozenlist/pull/678) |  | | #32  | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/frozenlist/#files) |\n| ✅ | **[yarl](https://github.com/aio-libs/yarl)** | [🔗Issue](https://github.com/aio-libs/yarl/issues/1515) | [🔗PR](https://github.com/aio-libs/yarl/pull/1516) |  | | #33 | [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/yarl/#files) |\n| ✅ | **avro-python3** | - | - | - | - | - |  [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/avro-python3/#files) |\n| ✅ | **fastavro** | - | [🔗PR](https://github.com/fastavro/fastavro/pull/871) | - | QC: Upstreamed changes for publishing win-arm64 wheels | - |  [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/fastavro/#files) |\n| ✅ | **srsly** | - | - | - | - | - |  [🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/srsly/#files) |\n| ✅ | **msgspec** | - | - | - | - | - | [🔗PR](https://pypi.org/project/msgspec/#files)  |\n| ✅ | **[mypy](https://github.com/python/mypy)** | [🔗Issue](https://github.com/python/mypy/issues/18593) |  - | - | - | #35 |[🔗pypi](https://pypi.org/project/mypy/#files) |\n| ⬛ | **ruamel-yaml-clib** | - | - | - | development has been stopped, instead use [ruamel.yaml.clibz](https://pypi.org/project/ruamel.yaml.clibz/#files)  | - | - |\n| ⬛ | **typed-ast** | - | - | - | [deprecated package, use native ast](https://github.com/python/typed_ast/issues/179) | - | - |\n| ⬛ | **fastparquet** | - | - | - | Require Panda 2.x, but Win-ARM64 supports from 3.x. Project retired. | - | - |\n| ⬛ | **gym** | - | - | - | deprecated package, use [gymnasium](https://pypi.org/project/gymnasium/#files)| - | - |\n\nTemplate:\n| Done | Tool | Issue | PR | Depend | Notes | Assigned | ETA/Wheel link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | \n| ⬛/❌/🟡/ ✅ | **[TEMPLATE]()** |  [🔗Issue]() | [🔗PR]() | DEPENDS | NOTES | ASSIGNED | ETA |","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmyznikov/PyEnv-WoA-State/issues/1/reactions","total_count":3,"+1":3,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/khmyznikov/PyEnv-WoA-State/issues/1/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180/events","html_url":"https://github.com/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180","id":4085777498,"node_id":"I_kwDORGgqa87ziARa","number":2180,"title":"[BOUNTY: 5 RTC] Create a YouTube or BoTTube video tutorial about any Elyan Labs project","user":{"login":"Scottcjn","id":121303252,"node_id":"U_kgDOBzrw1A","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/121303252?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn","html_url":"https://github.com/Scottcjn","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Scottcjn/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":10115035859,"node_id":"LA_kwDORGgqa88AAAACWucy0w","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/labels/bounty","name":"bounty","color":"0E8A16","default":false,"description":"RTC-paid bounty"}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":354,"created_at":"2026-03-17T02:05:49Z","updated_at":"2026-07-07T03:28:21Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"OWNER","active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## Bounty: 5 RTC\n\n### What to do\n1. Record a short video (2+ minutes) about any Elyan Labs project:\n   - Install and run the RustChain miner\n   - Upload a video to BoTTube via the API\n   - Use TrashClaw with a local LLM\n   - Set up rustchain-mcp in Claude Code\n2. Upload to YouTube OR [bottube.ai](https://bottube.ai)\n3. Comment here with the link + wallet\n\n5 RTC per video. Video backlinks are the strongest SEO signal.\n\n<!-- machine-readable bounty spec -->\n```bounty-spec\npaid: true\nreward_rtc: 5\nper: one-time\ncap: 1\nsubmit: [pr, email]\n```","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180/reactions","total_count":12,"+1":6,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":1,"confused":0,"heart":2,"rocket":2,"eyes":1},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Scottcjn/rustchain-bounties/issues/2180/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111/events","html_url":"https://github.com/logos-co/rfp/issues/111","id":4824958594,"node_id":"I_kwDOREPhhc8AAAABH5cGgg","number":111,"title":"[PROPOSAL] RFP-020 — RedStone Off-Chain Oracle Adaptor for LEZ","user":{"login":"kamikazebr","id":691510,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MTUxMA==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/691510?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr","html_url":"https://github.com/kamikazebr","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kamikazebr/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":10681272490,"node_id":"LA_kwDOREPhhc8AAAACfKdIqg","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/labels/proposal","name":"proposal","color":"8b91be","default":false,"description":"Proposal submitted for an RFP"},{"id":11152668839,"node_id":"LA_kwDOREPhhc8AAAACmMA4pw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/labels/RFP-020","name":"RFP-020","color":"0E8A16","default":false,"description":"Proposals for RFP-020"}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"hackyguru","id":66196424,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY2MTk2NDI0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/66196424?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru","html_url":"https://github.com/hackyguru","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false}],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-07-07T03:27:42Z","updated_at":"2026-07-07T03:27:49Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":{"login":"hackyguru","id":66196424,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY2MTk2NDI0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/66196424?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru","html_url":"https://github.com/hackyguru","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hackyguru/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### RFP ID\n\nRFP-020 — RedStone Off-Chain Oracle Adaptor for LEZ\n\n### Your Project Name\n\nRedStone Off-Chain Oracle Adaptor for LEZ\n\n### Team or Organization Name\n\nInfinityBase\n\n### Primary Contact\n\nvinicius@infinitybase.com\n\n### Team Members\n\n**Member 1**\nName: Vinicius Henrique Cadorin\nSocial links: https://www.linkedin.com/in/viniciuscadorin/ · https://www.infinitybase.com/\nRole: Project lead and primary contact (InfinityBase CEO). Owns delivery, timeline and coordination with the Logos team.\nStatus: part-time (project oversight)\n\n**Member 2**\nName: Guilherme Roque\nSocial links: https://github.com/guimroque\nRole: Technical lead (InfinityBase CTO). Owns architecture direction and review. Rust/Sway/Solidity smart-contract engineer. Built Bako Safe, the non-custodial multisig wallet native to Fuel Network, the first to ship passkey (WebAuthn) access without seed phrases while keeping self-custody, with 9,000+ accounts, and trusted with Fuel Network's own treasury. Directs the InfinityBase engineering organization (about 20 people).\nStatus: part-time (technical review)\n\n**Member 3**\nName: Felipe Novaes Rocha\nSocial links: https://github.com/kamikazebr\nRole: Engineering lead, protocol and security. Smart-contract auditor with a strong EVM background and years of production smart-contract work in the 1Hive ecosystem, on Gardens (https://gardens.fund) and Celeste. Runs a Logos L1 node since May 2026.\nStatus: part-time\n\n**Member 4**\nName: Jonatan Bolsoni\nSocial links: https://github.com/jonatanbolsoni\nRole: DevOps and infrastructure. Relayer deployment, monitoring and the ongoing feed operation.\nStatus: part-time (infrastructure and operations)\n\nThe project is delivered by InfinityBase, an engineering organization of about 20 people.\n\n### Project Summary\n\nExternal price feeds are needed by most DeFi applications planned on the LEZ. Lending, stablecoins and an exchange all need USD reference prices to work. RFP-020 chooses a good first oracle. RedStone covers privacy assets (XMR and ZEC), which is not common, it uses a self-serve connector model with no bridge dependency, and it uses a single verification primitive (secp256k1 ECDSA and keccak256) that the LEZ can run in-program today.\n\nInfinityBase delivers both consumption modes over one shared verification core. \n\nA public-mode push aggregator owns a canonical price account for the day-one BTC/ETH/SOL/XMR/ZEC-USD feeds.\n\nA public-mode pull library lets consumer programs verify signed payloads inline. The delivery also includes a TypeScript SDK, a CLI, and the compute-unit cost report that the RFP scopes as a primary deliverable, for the push write side and the per-read pull path.\n\nBefore writing this proposal we built a working proof of concept of the core path and checked the questions the RFP itself calls decisive. \n\nThe verification pipeline runs against live RedStone data for all five day-one feeds. \n\nThe cost profile was measured on the LEZ public-execution path. \n\nThe aggregator program was deployed and run end to end against a live standalone sequencer, and the price was read back correctly. \n\nThe PoC repository is private and we are happy to give the Logos team access on request: https://github.com/kamikazebr/lez-redstone-poc\n\nWe are also set up to operate this after delivery, not only build it. InfinityBase has team members already running Logos L1 nodes through the v0.2 release. \n\nWe commit to operating the day-one feeds under a servicing engagement after delivery, with a 99.9% SLA for one year after mainnet launch. The conditions are described under Post-Delivery Plan.\n\n### Technical Approach\n\n### Architecture\n\n<img width=\"1955\" height=\"1435\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b48ed629-7f07-4102-b3b0-476e2db9e118\" />\n\nBoth paths share one verification core. In push mode the aggregator pays verification once per update and every reader benefits. In pull mode the consumer pays per read but gets the freshest price. The admin surface (feed registration, signer-set rotation) is small and gated.\n\n### Verification core\nsecp256k1 ECDSA and keccak256 over RedStone data packages, using the RISC0-accelerated `k256` (elliptic-curve operations routed through the `bigint2` accelerator) and `tiny-keccak` (keccak coprocessor). Using the vanilla crates instead costs millions of cycles, so the accelerated configuration is a hard requirement that we document. The verifier does M-of-N over the authorised signer set, median aggregation, a maxAge freshness check, and dedup and overflow guards. It sits behind a `RedstoneVerifier` trait, so swapping in a future host precompile is a small, local change and not a rewrite.\nWe have validated end to end, against live RedStone data, that the five day-one feeds (including XMR/USD and ZEC/USD) are served by the same DeFi-grade five-signer set, and that our verification recovers the exact authorised signers.\n\n### Measured public-execution fit\nWe measured the cost profile on the LEZ public-execution path (same executor semantics and the same per-invocation session limit as `Program::execute()`, with `RISC0_DEV_MODE=0`), using real packages from RedStone production gateways:\n\n| Operation | CU | % of the per-invocation budget |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Single secp256k1 + keccak256 verification | 342,537 | 1.02% |\n| Synthetic 3-of-5 signature verification | 989,668 | 2.95% |\n| Full push write (decode 5 real packages, 3-of-5, median, account update) | 3,249,020 | 9.68% |\n| Pull per-read (5 packages) | 3,122,004 | 9.30% |\n| Aggregator price-account read | 111,468 | 0.33% |\n\nThese numbers match the known private-execution baseline, which confirms that public and private execution differ in proving, not in execution cost. A full price update fits in one public transaction with about 10x of headroom. The read is about 28x cheaper than a pull, and a push write is paid back after two reads, which is the basis for the \"when to use push vs pull\" guide. The per-package cost is linear (about 616K CU). The signer-recovery cost is about 1.8x a known-key verification, and that is the part a future secp256k1 and keccak256 precompile would remove, which is the input the RFP asks for the follow-on decision. Full details are in the cost report (M0). The PoC behind these numbers is available on request.\n\n### Push aggregator, pull library, reference consumers\nA public-mode LEZ program owns a canonical price account per feed. Registration and signer-set updates are admin-gated, with an anti-rollback check on accepted updates. The pull library does not depend on the aggregator (a consumer can integrate without registering a feed) and it never accepts a signer set from the payload. This is enforced by the instruction layout, not by a runtime check. Two reference consumers (aggregator-read and pull) show the refuse-on-unavailable pattern, and we tested adversarial cases such as valid signatures from keys outside the set, and a copy of the price account under an attacker program. Both are refused.\n\n### Relayer\nA headless daemon packaged as a Logos module. It supports a configurable dataServiceId (default `redstone-primary-prod`), per-feed heartbeat and deviation triggers, parallel fetch from multiple gateways with first-success, retry with back-off, structured logging with the on-chain rejection reason, wallet-balance monitoring, clean shutdown, and a documented operator flow. The operator documentation also surfaces the RedStone Terms of Use, since whoever runs the relayer is the party bound by them. The suggested starting values are a 0.5% deviation threshold and a 1-hour heartbeat. These are tunable defaults, not fixed contract values. Each asset has its own volatility and attack profile, so the internal price check runs much more often (seconds), and the final per-feed values will be set from backtesting and stress tests before we fix them.\n\n### Acceptance bar\nWe hold ourselves to the RFP's own bar: deploy and test on LEZ devnet/testnet, document per-operation compute-unit cost, demo the real end-to-end path against a live sequencer with RISC0_DEV_MODE=0, and end-to-end-against-sequencer integration tests in CI, green on the default branch. Built against a pinned LEZ v0.2.0.\n\n### Standards alignment and expected challenges\n- RFP-001 (admin authority): we integrate the standardised admin authority library through SPEL. If the library is not available at integration time, admin gating is done directly, with a documented small swap to the canonical library when it ships.\n- RFP-019 (price-account standard) is still changing. The account layout is isolated in one serialization module with version-tagged, append-only fields, following the fallback the RFP itself gives.\n- LEZ API churn. We build against pinned v0.2.0 and plan time for re-adaptation.\n- The Logos module tooling (module-builder, Basecamp) moves fast, and two of our deliverables sit on it: the relayer packaged as a Logos module and the mini-app. We pin the versions we build against, budget adaptation time, and follow the patterns used by the first-party modules.\n- Sequencer availability and the mainnet fee model are platform inputs for the servicing engagement. See Post-Delivery Plan.\n\n### Milestones, Payout and Timeline\n\n**Milestone 0 — Compute-unit cost report (gate review)**\nPayout: $2,000\nDuration: Days 1-3\nDeliverables: the public-mode cost report as a reproducible benchmark on a standalone LEZ sequencer, for both modes (push aggregator write and public-mode pull per-read). For each step (per-signer ECDSA recovery, keccak256 hashing, package decode, signer-set membership check, and for push mode the canonical price-account write and feed registration) we report compute units, RISC0 proof time, and RISC0 proof size, with a pinned toolchain, plus the per-mode cost delta versus a hypothetical native precompile. Numbers reproducible from the test suite. Gate-reviewed with the Logos team before M1 starts.\n\n**Milestone 1 — Verification core and push aggregator, deployed**\nPayout: $9,000\nDuration: Weeks 1-3\nDeliverables: the `redstone-verify` crate (decoder, verifier, signer-set, M-of-N, maxAge, typed errors), the `redstone-aggregator` program (canonical price account, admin-gated feed registration and signer-set update, anti-rollback), the SPEL IDL for the adaptor and the price account, deployed to LEZ devnet/testnet, with unit and integration tests and green CI.\n\n**Milestone 2 — Pull library, reference consumers, relayer, five feeds live**\nPayout: $9,000\nDuration: Weeks 3-7\nDeliverables: the public-mode pull library (does not depend on the aggregator, typed errors), two reference consumer programs (aggregator-read and pull, refuse-on-unavailable), the SPEL framework enhancement for push/pull feed hooks (a derive/attribute macro in the style of `#[admin_authority]`, with the reference consumers built on it as its acceptance test and a documented fallback if upstreaming does not land in the window), the relayer daemon with the full operator feature set, BTC/ETH/SOL/XMR/ZEC-USD registered and exercised, and end-to-end tests against the sequencer in CI.\n\n**Milestone 3 — SDK, CLI, mini-app, documentation, and security analysis**\nPayout: $10,000\nDuration: Weeks 7-10\nDeliverables: the TypeScript SDK (submit, read, pull dry-run) including the IDL codegen, the CLI for all core operations with a typed-error dry-run, and the Basecamp mini-app GUI (the off-chain feed dashboard): live price, configured signer set, M-of-N threshold, latest data-package timestamp and staleness per registered feed, plus a public-mode pull dry-run panel, delivered as a Logos module with local build instructions and loadable in Basecamp, together with the Figma designs for the dashboard. Also a README plus operator and consumer docs, the SDK and CLI doc packets for the Logos docs process, including the \"when to use push vs pull\" guide based on the cost report. Also the security analysis required by the RFP (AS3): the recommended production maxAge for both modes with the derivation, and a manipulation analysis (signer compromise, stale-package replay, signer-set update delays, and the pull-side job of keeping the signer set fresh), plus an adversarial test suite and a residual-risk register. An external third-party audit is available as a follow-on that Logos can commission (not included in this budget).\n\n---\n\n**Out of scope, available as follow-ons:** private-execution pull (blocked upstream on a signature-verification accelerator, per the RFP) and the secp256k1 and keccak256 precompile, for which this project's cost measurement is the input.\n\n---\n\n### Requirements coverage\n\nEvery hard requirement from the RFP's Scope of Work, mapped to the milestone that delivers it.\n\n**Functionality**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Push aggregator; precompile-swappable verification | M1 | Verifier isolated behind a swap-friendly boundary; one localized change when a precompile lands |\n| F2 | Verification packaged as a shared library (both modes) | M1 | redstone-verify; a first implementation already exists, 26 tests passing |\n| F3 | M-of-N threshold, reject below | M1 | Implemented and tested pre-submission (3-of-5 against real packages) |\n| F4 | Decode packages; reject stale/zero/invalid/mismatched | M1 | Decoder implemented; tamper/stale/mismatch rejections already tested |\n| F5 | Canonical price account per RFP-019 | M1 | Forward-compatible struct if RFP-019 unshipped, per the RFP's own fallback |\n| F6 | Admin authority per RFP-001 via SPEL | M1 | Integrated through SPEL; documented direct fallback if the library is not available at integration time |\n| F7 | 5 feeds registered on devnet/testnet | M2 | All five already validated live against the gateways pre-submission |\n| F8 | Relayer: Logos module + headless daemon, full operator feature set | M2 | Triggers, multi-gateway first-success, retry/back-off, logging, wallet monitoring, clean shutdown, operator docs incl. RedStone Terms of Use |\n| F9 | Pull verification library, aggregator-independent, typed errors | M2 | Same shared core; gateway-IP privacy caveat documented |\n\n**Usability**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| U1 | SDK: submit / read / pull | M3 | TypeScript SDK, including building the IDL codegen |\n| U2 | Basecamp mini-app GUI | M3 | Feed dashboard: prices, signer set, threshold, staleness, plus a pull dry-run panel |\n| U3 | CLI for both modes incl. pull dry-run | M3 | Same typed error codes as on-chain |\n| U4 | IDL via SPEL | M1 | Adaptor + price account (re-exported from RFP-019) |\n| U5 | SPEL enhancement for push/pull hooks | M2 | Derive/attribute macro; reference consumers built on it as its acceptance test; documented fallback if upstream stalls |\n| U6 | Actionable typed errors for all failure modes | M1-M2 | Already the design of redstone-verify (typed error enum, no panics) |\n| U7 | Two reference consumer programs | M2 | Aggregator-read + pull, refuse-on-unavailable pattern; first cuts already built and measured, incl. adversarial tests |\n\n**Reliability**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | Price read is read-only | M1 | Architectural invariant |\n| R2 | Atomic feed registration | M1 | Partial failure leaves existing registrations intact |\n| R3 | Per-feed error isolation | M2 | One failing upstream feed never blocks the others |\n| R4 | Daemon recovers from transient upstream errors | M2 | Back-off and auto-resume |\n\n**Performance**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | Single-tx 3-of-N verification, both modes | M0 | Already validated pre-submission: complete real-package 3-of-5 pipeline at 9.3% of the budget |\n| P2 | Cost measurement as primary deliverable, reproducible | M0-M1 | Both modes, per-operation breakdown (CU, proof time, proof size), reproducible from the test suite |\n| P3 | Cost delta vs hypothetical precompile, per mode | M1 | Against EVM reference points (RedStone 50-100K gas end-to-end) |\n\n**Supportability**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | Deployed on LEZ devnet/testnet | M1-M2 | Aggregator at M1; feeds + relayer at M2 |\n| S2 | E2E tests against sequencer in CI, green on default branch | M2-M3 | Already exercised pre-submission against a live standalone sequencer |\n| S3 | At least 1 test per hard requirement, both modes, incl. boundary cases | M1-M3 | Pull tests run inside the reference consumer, not just unit tests |\n| S4 | README: end-to-end usage for both modes | M3 | |\n| S5 | SDK doc packet (developer journey + consumer pattern + push-vs-pull guide) | M3 | Grounded in the cost measurement |\n| S6 | CLI doc packet | M3 | |\n| S7 | Figma designs for the mini-app | M3 | Delivered with the mini-app |\n\n**Adaptor Security**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AS1 | Reject unauthorized signers, both modes | M1-M2 | Membership check already implemented and tested |\n| AS2 | Signer-set updates admin-only (push); pull never trusts payload-sourced signer sets | M1-M2 | Enforced by construction in the instruction layout; update path tested |\n| AS3 | Recommended maxAge + manipulation analysis | M3 | Signer compromise, replay, signer-set update delays, pull-side freshness responsibility; first full draft already written |\n\n**Soft Requirements**\n| # | Requirement | Milestone | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Soft1 | Multi-feed batched verification | M2 (best-effort) | Amortise decoding and recovery across feeds in one instruction |\n| Soft2 | Multi-source test with RFP-019 TWAP | Follow-on | Once the TWAP program exists |\n\n### Total Requested Budget (USD)\n\n$30,000\n\n### Relevant Experience\n\n**Company: InfinityBase**\nA product studio founded in 2018, headquartered in Florianópolis, Brazil, and operating globally. We have shipped 40+ products across blockchain infrastructure and applications (smart-contract and protocol engineering in Rust, Sway and Solidity, full-stack dApp development, and DeFi infrastructure) with a security-first process and recurring engagements with independent audit firms.\nWebsite: https://www.infinitybase.com/ · GitHub: https://github.com/infinitybase · LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/infinitybase/\n\nSelected work:\n- Bako Safe (https://www.bako.global/): the native multisig of Fuel Network, audited by QuillAudits. It secures the Fuel network's treasury, having safeguarded $40M+ in assets for 9,000+ users at the network's peak.\n- Vero (https://vero.finance/): cross-border payments and credit platform for importers and exporters, incubated inside InfinityBase and now a standalone company operating multi-currency, real-money flows under strict compliance.\n- Silhouette: Shield Exchange built on Hyperliquid for private on-chain execution, backed by a $3M pre-seed round (RockawayX, Amber Group, NGC Ventures). InfinityBase drove the technology end to end: functional demo integrated with Hyperliquid in 2.5 weeks, Closed Beta in 2 months, and full Mainnet launch in 4.5 months.\n\n**Logos-specific track record:**\n- Working proof of concept for this exact RFP (verification core, on-chain aggregator with reference consumers, relayer, cost measurements, security analysis, and an end-to-end run against a live standalone sequencer). Private repository, access on request: https://github.com/kamikazebr/lez-redstone-poc\n- Protocol contribution accepted by the Logos core team. While operating our own L1 node we found, measured and reported a leader-liveness degradation (per-epoch leader compute scaling with the staking key's UTXO note count, to the point of forfeiting the whole election). Report with measurements: https://gist.github.com/kamikazebr/5353c848528f303a60c9e8326ae9cea4. The core team confirmed it and the synchronous scan was removed in https://github.com/logos-blockchain/logos-blockchain/pull/2959, shipped in rc11. At their request we produced the measurement study used to set the recommended node hardware baseline.\n- Critical bug found and fixed in lez-multisig (the Logos multisig sample application): the executor could change the payout target after approvals. Fix with 29 tests submitted upstream: https://github.com/logos-co/lez-multisig/pull/41\n- Team members run Logos L1 nodes through the v0.1 to v0.2 migration, including state recovery and release testing.\n\n**Broader track record:**\n- Production on-chain funding systems in the 1Hive ecosystem: Gardens (https://gardens.fund), a conviction-voting public-goods funding platform, and Celeste, its decentralized dispute-resolution protocol. Years of work shipping mechanisms that hold live funds, directly relevant to building oracle infrastructure other projects' funds depend on.\n\n### Post-Delivery Plan\n\nInfinityBase operates the day-one feeds after delivery under a servicing engagement, with a 99.9% monthly uptime SLA for one year after mainnet launch. The service also runs during the testnet period before mainnet, but the strict SLA and its penalties only apply after mainnet launch, which gives the whole stack (RedStone and the Logos chain included) time to mature. This is covered by a separate infrastructure and support fee of $1,000 per month. The fee covers the dedicated infrastructure (two redundant relayer nodes with monitoring on price staleness, gas-wallet balance and process liveness), the on-chain gas for price updates, and an on-call engineer to handle incidents, with runbooks and monthly operating reports.\n\nThe SLA covers the relayer and gateway service we operate. Three conditions are outside our control and count as excused downtime, and we state them for transparency. First, RedStone publishes no SLA of its own, so an outage or a breaking change on their side that we cannot fix within a 48-minute monthly maintenance window would reach us. Second, if the Logos chain is unavailable we cannot write updates. Third, the mainnet fee model is not published yet, so we propose treating on-chain fees as a reimbursable pass-through, so neither side carries the fee-model risk. We will settle the operating details with the Logos team, together with a committed public sequencer RPC endpoint, before the engagement begins.\n\nOne suggestion for the road to mainnet. Today the update path runs on machines whose keys we hold. For a stronger guarantee against market manipulation, we suggest that as mainnet gets closer, independent organizations run additional nodes, so the update path is shared and not held by one operator. We are happy to design that transition (for example a multi-signature scheme over the update path) as a follow-on.\n\n### Permissions and Consent\n\n- [x] I confirm Logos may contact me using the primary contact information provided above for follow-ups and next steps.\n- [x] I consent to Logos using information from this proposal publicly such as blogs case studies social posts or analytical reporting. Redactions can be requested at any time.\n\n### Program Requirements\n\n- [x] We understand this project must be open-sourced under the MIT and Apache 2.0 Licenses unless explicitly approved otherwise.\n- [x] We are prepared to deliver milestone-based outcomes.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/logos-co/rfp/issues/111/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null,"score":1.0},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/issues/94108","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/issues/94108/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/issues/94108/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/issues/94108/events","html_url":"https://github.com/Expensify/App/issues/94108","id":4705923271,"node_id":"I_kwDOEQqHYc8AAAABGH6wxw","number":94108,"title":"[Due for payment 2026-07-03] [$250] Travel-\"What's your legal name\" page instead of \"You'll need to use your work email\" message","user":{"login":"applause-bot[bot]","id":265651874,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOD9WGog","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/3009811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/applause-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/applause-bot%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[{"id":2873246102,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwyODczMjQ2MTAy","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/labels/External","name":"External","color":"a7b8ef","default":false,"description":"Added to denote the issue can be worked on by a contributor"},{"id":2893455829,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwyODkzNDU1ODI5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/labels/Daily","name":"Daily","color":"eb6420","default":false,"description":"KSv2"},{"id":3271255930,"node_id":"MDU6TGFiZWwzMjcxMjU1OTMw","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/labels/Awaiting%20Payment","name":"Awaiting Payment","color":"ededed","default":false,"description":"Auto-added when associated PR is deployed to production"},{"id":4664850679,"node_id":"LA_kwDOEQqHYc8AAAABFgv49w","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Expensify/App/labels/Bug","name":"Bug","color":"93074F","default":false,"description":"Something is broken. Auto assigns a BugZero manager."}],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[{"login":"arosiclair","id":6833644,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY4MzM2NDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6833644?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair","html_url":"https://github.com/arosiclair","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/arosiclair/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/